    {
      "say": "Here is the subtle difference. On the left, allusion: an author deliberately points to a specific source, and the reader spots it. On the right, intertextuality is wider: every text is woven from earlier language, genres and texts, whether the author meant it or not. Roland Barthes argued in 1967 that the reader, not the author, makes meaning. Which gives readers more power?",
